Ladybird resolves filter regions and primitive subregions as Chrome 157
and Firefox 151 do, verified at runtime on 24 cases: default, fractional
and percentage subregions in both units, zero widths, feImage fitting,
and the filter region on SVG and HTML elements. The two differ on a
negative width: Chrome disables the primitive, as the spec says, and
Ladybird does too, while Firefox ignores the attribute. WebKit
(Playwright’s 26.5 build) treats a zero-size primitive as an error that
hides the element, so it’s no reference here.

For an HTML element, Blink’s PaintLayer::UpdateFilterReferenceBox()
takes LocalBoundingBoxIncludingSelfPaintingDescendants(), the box’s
visual overflow grown by its self-painting descendants, and its
ComputeFilterViewport() takes the box’s size for userSpaceOnUse
percentages. Ladybird has no visual overflow, so it grows the border box
by the scrollable overflow and the descendants’ border boxes instead.
All three engines render filter-region-transformed-child-001 and
filter-region-negative-positioned-child-001 that way.
